'''''Leucocasia gigantea''''', also called the '''giant elephant ear''' or '''Indian taro''', is a species of [[flowering plant]]. It is a {{cvt|1.5–3|m}} tall [[Araceae|aroid]] plant with a large, fibrous [[corm]], producing at its apex a [[Whorl (botany)|whorl]] of thick, green leaves.<ref>Anton Ivancic et al. [http://www.fkbv.uni-mb.si/botvrt/documents/Colocasia.pdf Thermogenesis and flowering biology of Colocasia gigantea, Araceae] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131029233619/http://www.fkbv.uni-mb.si/botvrt/documents/Colocasia.pdf |date=2013-10-29 }} J Plant Res (2008) 121:73–82.</ref> It is the sole species in genus ''Leucocasia''.<ref name = powo/>

==Cultivation==
''Leucocasia gigantea'' is a "sister species" to another widely-cultivated 'taro', [[Taro|''Colocasia esculenta'']], as well as to the [[Alocasia|alocasias]], such as the large ''[[Alocasia macrorrhizos]]''; it is speculated that ''L. gigantea'' was created as a result of natural hybridization between ''A. macrorrhizos'' and ''C. esculenta''.<ref>{{cite web |title=The global diversity of Taro: ethnobotany and conservation |url=https://bioversityinternational.org/fileadmin/_migrated/uploads/tx_news/The_global_diversity_of_taro__ethnobotany_and_conservation_1402.pdf |access-date=30 December 2021 |archive-date=21 December 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211221195807/https://bioversityinternational.org/fileadmin/_migrated/uploads/tx_news/The_global_diversity_of_taro__ethnobotany_and_conservation_1402.pdf |url-status=dead }}</ref> It is called '<nowiki/>''dọc mùng''<nowiki/>' in [[northern Vietnam]] and '<nowiki/>''môn bạc hà''<nowiki/>' or '''bạc hà''<nowiki/>' in some provinces in [[southern Vietnam]].<ref>Nguyen Thi Ngoc Hue 'Taro diversity and use in Vietnam'. [https://web.archive.org/web/20140714210257/http://ir.minpaku.ac.jp/dspace/bitstream/10502/4766/1/MP_GDT.pdf Ethnobotany and genetic diversity of Asian taro: focus on China]</ref><ref>{{Deprecated archive|url=https://archive.today/20130422022643/http://www.vietgle.vn/trithucviet/detail.aspx?pid=NzhDRjBFMEEwNQ&key=Lo%C3%A0i+M%C3%B4n+to,+D%E1%BB%8Dc+m%C3%B9ng&type=A0&stype=0|title=Loài Dọc mùng}} Tri thức việt - Vietgle.</ref><ref>Matsuda M, Nawata E; "Taro in Northern Vietnam : Its Uses, Cultivation, and Genetic Variation" Japanese Journal of Tropical Agriculture, VOL.46;NO.4;PAGE.247-258(2002)</ref>

In [[Japanese language|Japanese]], it is commonly called ハス芋 (''hasu-imo''),<ref>Nguyen Thi Ngoc Hue [https://web.archive.org/web/20140714210257/http://ir.minpaku.ac.jp/dspace/bitstream/10502/4766/1/MP_GDT.pdf 'Taro diversity and use in Vietnam' Ethnobotany and genetic diversity of Asian taro: focus on China]</ref> or "lotus yam". It is known as ''ryukyu'' in [[Kōchi Prefecture]], as it is found in the [[Ryukyu Kingdom]].{{cn|date=July 2024}}

==Uses==
In addition to its value as a starchy root vegetable—known by many names, such as ''taro'', or ''arbi'' (in [[Hindi]])—the plant’s [[Petiole (botany)|leaf stalk]] (petiole) is also used as a vegetable in some areas of [[South East Asia|Southeastern Asia]] and [[Japan]].{{cn|date=July 2024}} It is sometimes used as an ingredient in [[miso soup|''miso'' soup]], ''[[chanpurū]]'' and [[sushi]].{{cn|date=July 2024}}

